Shoals.Stochastic: kou jump parameters must be finite
Assumption: the jump-count table needs a finite jump multiplier
w = E[exp(Y)] = 1 + zeta, and eta_up <= 1 does not give it one -- the up-jump
exponential moment integral diverges. sto_kou_compensator returns a NaN
sentinel in that case and always has; what changed in shoals#132 is that the
sampler no longer multiplies that NaN into its drift and returns NaN for every
path.

This guard and its Merton counterpart are the same predicate reached by two
different routes, which is why the finiteness check lives in
count_params_finite rather than in either model. Merton's non-finite
log_w comes from a non-finite INPUT; Kou's comes from a divergent moment of
perfectly finite ones. A reader looking only at Merton's fixture would
conclude the check is about input validation. It is not.

Deleting this guard clause does NOT make the fixture pass. Measured on this
pin: it then fails with the SLOT CAP diagnostic instead, because exp(NaN) is
NaN, `lt(nan, 1.0)` is false so the tilted mean stays NaN, and
`not(lte(nan, 4096.0))` is true. `--expect neg` sees the wrong text and the
file fails as WRONG-DIAGNOSTIC rather than passing; the assertion is never
reached. That is the whole point of pairing a required diagnostic with the
fixture: a guard removal has to change the TEXT, not merely keep something
failing.
